    "result_text": "The build is green. The full suite (the acceptance tests are the only test classes in the project) passes.\n\n## Summary\n\nI changed telephone handling to store and compare numbers in E.164 form.\n\n**`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`**\n- Added a `toE164(String)` helper that:\n  - strips spaces, dashes and brackets;\n  - keeps an explicit leading `+` and country code when present;\n  - otherwise assumes `+61` and drops a single leading `0` from the national digits;\n  - requires 8\u201315 digits after the `+`, returning `null` (\u2192 400) otherwise.\n- `addOwner` now normalizes to E.164, rejects unformattable numbers with 400, and detects duplicates by comparing E.164 values (existing owners are normalized through `toE164` too, so old-format seed data still matches correctly). The normalized string is stored/returned.\n- `updateOwner` applies the same normalization and 400 rejection for consistency.\n\n**`Owner.java`**\n- Replaced the old `@Digits(integer=10)` + `@Pattern(\"^[0-9]{10}$\")` entity constraints with `@Pattern(\"^\\\\+[0-9]{8,15}$\")` matching E.164, and removed the now-unused `Digits` import.\n\nVerified behavior against the tests: `0412 345 678` \u2192 `+61412345678`, `+64 21 123 456` \u2192 `+6421123456`, and `12` \u2192 400. `VARCHAR(20)` columns comfortably hold the E.164 value.",
